TEXT -96 88 Left 2 !.tran 0 7s 0 0.6ms
TEXT -96 96 Left 2 !.param tdv=1 tdt=1 fn1=50 omega_n={2*pi*fn1}\n.options vntol=10 abstol=100m reltol=0.003
TEXT 272 104 Left 2 ;General:\n \nThis model is a synchronous generator with transformer and voltage(AVR),speed regulator\n \n \nparams:\n                \n            Schenkelpolfactor=0.5 for salient pole machine (kc=0.8-1.0)\n                                              1 for cylindrical pole machine(kc=0.4-0.5)                              \n             Daempferfaktor=1 (damping factor)\n             Streuung=0.05 (leakage factor)\n             rcu=0.01 (rel. Copper resistance)\n             rbg=0.001 (rel. friction moment)\n             J=100000 (inertia)\n             a=0.9 parameter for nonlinear saturation\n             m=7 parameter for nonlinear saturation \n             n0=1500 Starting speed\n             err0=0 (if 1 full starting voltage but set starting timepoint in .TRAN card approx. 10ms to prevent spikes)\n             t_start_avr=2s The ON switch to the AVR \n             t_stop_avr=1e6 The OFF switch of the AVR (now external regulation is possible via F1 Pin)\n             t_enable_avr=2.0s PI-Reglulator is active ( should be same time as  t_start_avr)\n             t_enablev_avr=1.5s Voltage measurement for AVR  is started (should be before enable_avr)\n             t_stop_fixerr=2.5s Excitement of field voltage with PWL-source finished (shortlly before should be  t_start_avr)\n             t_start_fixerr=1e6 The external regulation of the field voltage is on (shortly behind should be t_stop_avr)\n             t_stop_nreg=10e5 The speed regulator is off. Now one can with t_start_fixm (should be shortly before t_stop_nreg)\n                                            switch to the external torque regulation at the M-pin as PWL-source (only for operation on a network \n                                            with voltagesource and not on a isolated network!!!!)\n             t_start_nreg=10e6 The speed regulator is on. Shortly after this time should be t_stop_fixm \n             t_start_fixm=1e5 The speed regulator should be stopped shortly after  t_start_fixm (with t_stop_nreg). This make sense if\n                                        the generator is synchronized to a network, now the speed regulator should end and the output power of\n                                        the generator is changed by changing the torque with a external PWL-source\n             t_stop_fixm=1e6 The external torque control is off, Now the speed controller should be on shortly before ( t_start_nreg) \n             t_sync=1200ms Switch the synchronous machine and the transformer to the network         \n             t_stop_gnd=1e5 Switch OFF the generator star point (default ON) \n             t_start_gnd=1e6 Switch ON the generator star point  \n \nAlternative elements: (changed via doubleclick on the Spicemodelline)\n          \nHINT:  The output power can be traced by the formula M*314.159/2  (P=M*omega/p)\n                     The load angle can be traced by V(u1:u1:theta) \n                     The frequency is n/30  2*n/60 = p*n/60 (p=Polcount)\n                     The very simple speed regulator in this model is ideal so it has no timedelay and steplimit\n                     This behaviour is valid most likely at a hydraulic turbine (never a thermal block) \n \nLibfile: Sgen.lib
TEXT 16 256 Left 2 !.param RN=0.4 LN={4/314.159}
TEXT 152 376 Left 2 !.param PN=100e6 QN=0
TEXT -272 344 Left 2 !Verr0 verr 0 pwl 0s 1800v 0.7s 600v
TEXT 8 80 Left 2 ;Reltol is set to 0.005, vntol to 100m \nfor convergence
TEXT -96 320 Left 2 ;Isolator model for a valid\nhigh ohmic Path to earth
TEXT -184 408 Left 4 ;Generator on a Island (isolated network)\nAVR+Governer are ON
TEXT -184 456 Left 2 ;The speed control is ON at the beginning and rests ON\nThe voltage regulation is externally at startup Verr0 and with\ntime > 2s the voltage is regulated by AVR\nSince there is a isolated network the speed controller must be ON the whole time\nAT 1200ms the Generator is switched to the network\nAt 2s there is a load step with 100MW
TEXT 136 296 Left 2 ;Load step\nsee M,n
